	One objection already raised against Nowak's argument has to do with concerns of linguistic transfer through time; call this the generational transfer objection. The generational transfer objection is similar to the critique I will offer here of Nowak's view, so it will be useful to discuss the objection as it is originally articulated in his paper here.
	The issue raised by the generational transfer objection, in general terms, is that considerations regarding social identity and autonomy possible only in one's first language may generate reasons to protect current speakers' access to their first language communities, but these reasons do not necessarily translate into reasons why we should ensure that future speakers continue to speak any particular language as opposed to another. Whichever language future speakers are raised in will be their first language- so long as all speakers retain access to whatever their first language happens to be, it does not then matter whether a people's first language ends up changing across different generations. One reviewer puts forward a hypothetical scenario (involving specifically English and French) of a linguistic assimilative policy enacted on a community, which is carried out in such a way that it never leaves any member of any generation without lifelong access to her first language, but that nevertheless eventually achieves a full shift of the first language in that community to the assimilative target language. The challenge, then, is that taking into account only those reasons for linguistic preservation that Nowak has given, such a linguistic shift would be an acceptable outcome as long as it is done so that it does not at any point in time violate any speaker's interest in being able to keep speaking her first language.
	It is useful to reproduce Nowak's response to the generational transfer objection here:
	"...Even if we set aside questions about whether there could be defensible reasons for implementing a curriculum expressly designed to assimilate a linguistic minority, it is hardly clear that the kids in this case will be ok as French fades and disappears. Of course, someone raised as a fully-bilingual speaker of French and English would be better off in a future society in which only English is spoken than a monolingual French speaker would be, or than an adult student of English as a second language. The bilingual person, after all, still has access to a linguistic community in which she counts as a fully-empowered member. If these bilingual students are raised as native French speakers by Francophone parents, [...] there are things these students can do when speaking French that they cannot do when speaking English, [...and] these people will end up with a substantial interest in preserving access to their Francophone communities. Without such access, they will be unable to be recognized, as it were, as the people they are. Since every generation will have a significant interest in sharing a native language with their children, and children an interest in sharing their parents' language, the kinds of claims to which linguistic identity gives rise are likely to endure through time." (26)
	The details of the original objector's English-French case are not reproduced in Nowak's essay, so it is not clear what the envisioned hypothetical assimilative process that he is responding to is actually like. 